Unapplied activation artifact: persistent-session dependency admission
===================================================================

Prepared against the active persistent-reap session.rs/session_supervisor.rs baseline.
The reentry guard stays in public connect before its owner task is spawned.
This file changes no runtime until explicitly applied.

Prerequisites:
- Link the broker modules/API/exports and include required build inputs separately.
- Runtime owner must use session::ensure_persistent_call_allowed() from
  connect_with_broker too. That new module already initializes broker: Some(...).
- Broker connection must revoke parent authority, join reverse work and its
  payload decoding, then release the context and publish the ordinary response.
- revoke_all must prevent subsequent scope admission, not only revoke current
  contexts. No new scope may survive terminal connection shutdown.

Admission is one transaction after both channel/slot reservations: candidate ID,
fallible broker.scope, then committed ID + Pending + infallible permit.send with
no await. A rejected scope consumes no forward ID or capacity. Accepted scope
state belongs to the broker until supervised parent completion, independent of
the response waiter. Scope expiry/cancellation fences dependencies, not the
already accepted forward operation.

Detached DeferredControl is deliberately unscoped. Its pre-authorized reserved
lane and after_sent gate remain valid when the parent waiter disappears; it
cannot reuse stale parent authority. release() rejects reentrant waiting, while
its Drop still queues the pre-authorized cleanup. Scoped cleanup is not offered.
The task-local guard cannot detect ancestry in spawned helper tasks; host service
implementations must obey the no-persistent-callback contract in those tasks too.

Seven admission/lifetime regression tests are included, not yet compiled/run.
These supplement, not replace, real broker process/close/saturation tests.
No Cargo/build command has been run for this artifact.

